```
ROLE: area_chair
FINAL_VERDICT: yellow
REVIEWER_VOTES: {novelty_killer: yellow, systems_reviewer: yellow, evaluation_prosecutor: yellow, theory_skeptic: yellow, product_realist: yellow}
FATAL_OBJECTIONS:
  - "Novel mechanism" framing is dead. The loud-vs-silent-by-corruption-location asymmetry is the documented, expected behavior of any length-prefixed/terminator-framed WAL with data-scoped checksums. RocksDB WAL Recovery Modes (kPointInTimeRecovery = stop at first corruption, open clean, discard rest) and LevelDB log_reader (truncated/short trailing record -> EOF, checksum mismatch -> error) both codify this exact dichotomy. Verified across ≥2 independent sources by novelty_killer, corroborated by systems_reviewer, theory_skeptic, and product_realist. The anti-hallucination rule is satisfied: the collision is real.
  - The reorder-SIM creates a POSIX-impossible crash state for SyncAll users. evaluation_prosecutor's objection is unrebutted: if fsync completed for batch N+1, POSIX guarantees batch N is also on disk. A zeroed interior page followed by valid fsync'd data is unreachable via OS crash on barrier-honoring storage. This renders the "12 SyncAll-acked keys silently discarded after the hole" result an artifact of manual injection, not a demonstrable crash vulnerability. No reviewer rebutted this.
  - The only REAL crash evidence (dm-flakey) drops un-fsync'd Buffer-mode data, which is documented-lossy behavior (writer.rs:232 = literal no-op). Silent loss of contractually-allowed-lossy data is not a durability violation — it is the contract working as specified.
REQUIRED_EVIDENCE:
  - dm-log-writes barrier-reorder replay: record a real SyncAll workload, replay to every crash point, demonstrate a point where SyncAll returned Ok for a key but recovery silently opens without it. This is the L1 gate. Without it, the post-fsync severity claim is unsubstantiated.
  - Peer-LSM differential: run the IDENTICAL injection harness (same corruption modes, same byte offsets) against RocksDB (with kPointInTimeRecovery) and sled/redb. If the asymmetry table is indistinguishable, the "fjall-specific" claim collapses to "known WAL behavior, fjall instance." If fjall is uniquely silent where peers are loud or offer opt-out, that is the real delta.
  - Repetition + variance: n=1 per corruption mode with a single 50-key run is insufficient. Sweep over corruption offset, post-hole batch count, and journal size. Report CIs on recovery counts.
  - Framing-checksum scope comparison: document whether RocksDB/LevelDB/etcd checksum framing metadata (record type, length) in addition to data. If peers checksum framing and fjall does not, THAT is the mechanism delta — not "location decides loud-vs-silent" generically.
MANDATORY_BASELINES:
  - RocksDB kPointInTimeRecovery under identical framing-corruption injection (the closest behavioral analog — must show whether RocksDB also opens clean and silently discards, or surfaces an error/warning)
  - LevelDB log_reader with paranoid_checks=false under identical injection (the ancestral format)
  - fjall with PersistMode::SyncAll under dm-log-writes barrier-reorder (the severity escalation test)
MAP_DELTA_PROPOSAL:
  {node: "CLAIM-0071", change: "HOLD at YELLOW. Do NOT advance to L1-GREEN. Reclassify contribution_type from 'novel mechanism' to 'un-owned instantiation of known WAL recovery behavior in a specific adopted system (fjall).' Severity capped at 'silent default without opt-out' until dm-log-writes L1 evidence crosses the durability-contract boundary. Add required_prior_art: [RocksDB WAL Recovery Modes, LevelDB log_format, Pillai OSDI'14 ALICE, Ganesan FAST'17]. Gate L1 advancement on: (1) dm-log-writes post-fsync silent loss reproduction, (2) peer-LSM differential under identical harness, (3) repetition with variance."}
PRIOR_ART_ADEQUATE: yes
RATIONALE:
  Unanimous YELLOW from all five reviewers. No vote is unrebutted; I aggregate by evidence, not count, but here evidence and count align.

  Stress-test A (folklore gate): RESOLVED AGAINST the claim's strongest framing. The novelty_killer identified — and three other reviewers independently corroborated — that the loud-vs-silent-by-corruption-location asymmetry is a documented property of the WAL format family fjall inherits. RocksDB's WAL Recovery Modes wiki (https://github.com/facebook/rocksdb/wiki/WAL-Recovery-Modes) explicitly parameterizes the exact behavior (tail-tolerant vs absolute-consistency vs point-in-time), meaning the design space is not merely known but already has a configurable API in a mature peer. LevelDB's log_reader treats truncated trailing records as EOF and checksum failures as errors — the same shape. The "novel mechanism" framing is therefore dead. What survives is narrower: fjall hardwires the silent-truncate behavior as the ONLY mode with no opt-out and no error surface, which is un-owned in fjall's issue tracker (#53 is cosmetic trailing-batch warning, #112/#113 are a different fixed bug, no CVE/Jepsen/paper). This is a legitimate empirical finding about one real system, but it is an INSTANTIATION of a known phenomenon, not a new phenomenon.

  Stress-test B (durability-contract boundary): RESOLVED AS THE HARD CAP. The evaluation_prosecutor's POSIX-barrier argument is decisive and unrebutted: if SyncAll's fsync completed for batch N+1, the kernel guarantees batch N is on stable storage. The reorder-SIM's zeroed-interior-page-with-valid-subsequent-data state is therefore unreachable via OS crash on barrier-honoring hardware. The only real crash evidence (dm-flakey) drops Buffer-mode data where the journal writer performs no fsync — this is contractually allowed loss, not a bug. Until dm-log-writes barrier-reorder replay demonstrates that a real device can produce an interior hole in fsync'd data (i.e., the device violates or reorders across the barrier), the post-fsync severity claim is unsubstantiated. The theory_skeptic correctly notes the sim "cannot distinguish 'fjall violates its durability contract' from 'we zeroed a page the device would never have lost post-fsync.'"

  What the claim IS, after committee review: a source-verified, un-owned report that fjall 3.1.4 ships a known-unsafe WAL recovery default (silent truncate on framing corruption, no AbsoluteConsistency opt-out, no error surface) with a clean discriminating control (in-data corruption is loud). The empirical work is methodologically sound at L0 (source verification, control-held asymmetry table, real dm-flakey crash). But the contribution is bounded: "this specific young system shipped the known-unsafe default without the known safety knob" — actionable for fjall maintainers/users, worth an issue or advisory, but not a publishable novel finding without (a) reframing away from mechanism novelty, (b) mandatory prior-art citation of RocksDB/LevelDB/ALICE/FAST'17, and (c) the L1 dm-log-writes evidence that would escalate severity from "documented-lossy mode is lossy" to "acknowledged-durable data silently lost."

  GREEN is not possible at this stage: the mechanism-novelty framing requires mandatory reframe + citation, the severity requires L1 evidence, and the "fjall-specific" claim requires a peer-LSM differential. No reviewer's YELLOW is overridable by evidence in the packet. Prompt version v001.
